---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const pageTitle = '48 Hours in Paso Robles | California Weekend Getaway';
const pageDescription =
  'A practical 48-hour Paso Robles itinerary with wine country, downtown dining, scenic stops, and time to relax.';
---

<Base title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <section>
    <p>48-Hour California Getaway</p>

    <h1>48 Hours in Paso Robles</h1>

    <h2>A relaxed California wine-country weekend with plenty to enjoy beyond the tasting room.</h2>

    <p>
      Paso Robles is one of those California destinations that fits naturally
      into a two-day escape. Vineyards roll across the surrounding hills,
      downtown is compact and inviting, and good food, wine, and scenery are
      never far apart.
    </p>

    <p>
      This itinerary isn't designed to squeeze everything Paso Robles offers
      into one weekend. Instead, it gives you a comfortable framework for
      enjoying the area without spending your 48 hours racing from one stop
      to another.
    </p>

    <h2>Friday Evening: Arrive and Settle In</h2>

    <p>
      If possible, arrive Friday afternoon or early evening. Check into your
      lodging and give yourself time to settle in before heading into downtown
      Paso Robles.
    </p>

    <p>
      Make your first evening simple: walk around the downtown square, have
      dinner nearby, and enjoy the atmosphere. Save the longer drives and
      winery visits for Saturday.
    </p>

    <h2>Saturday Morning: Start Slowly</h2>

    <p>
      Begin with breakfast or coffee in town before heading into Paso Robles
      wine country. There is no need to schedule an early morning tasting.
      Part of the appeal of Paso Robles is the landscape itself, so allow
      time for the drive.
    </p>

    <h2>Saturday: Explore Paso Robles Wine Country</h2>

    <p>
      Choose two or three wineries rather than trying to visit as many as
      possible. Reservations may be recommended or required, so confirm
      current tasting policies before your trip.
    </p>

    <p>
      Leave room between appointments. A relaxed tasting, a vineyard lunch,
      or simply sitting outside with a view can easily become one of the best
      parts of the weekend.
    </p>

    <h2>Saturday Evening: Back to Downtown</h2>

    <p>
      Return to your hotel before dinner and give yourself a little downtime.
      Downtown Paso Robles makes the evening easy, with restaurants, tasting
      rooms, shops, and the town square all within a relatively compact area.
    </p>

    <h2>Sunday Morning: One More Side of Paso</h2>

    <p>
      Sunday is a good time to see something beyond the wineries. Enjoy a
      leisurely breakfast, explore downtown, visit a local attraction, or
      take a scenic drive before beginning the trip home.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to Stay</h2>

    <p>
      Staying near downtown works especially well for a first 48-hour visit
      because restaurants and evening activities are close at hand. Travelers
      looking for a quieter experience can also consider lodging among the
      vineyards or elsewhere in the surrounding countryside.
    </p>

    <h2>Before You Go</h2>

    <p>
      Winery hours, tasting fees, restaurant schedules, and reservation
      requirements can change. Check directly with the places you plan to
      visit before leaving home, particularly on weekends and during busy
      travel periods.
    </p>

    <h2>Make the 48 Hours Yours</h2>

    <p>
      Think of this itinerary as a framework rather than a checklist.
      Paso Robles rewards travelers who leave a little room in the schedule
      for an unexpected tasting, a long lunch, a scenic road, or simply
      another hour enjoying the view.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two days won't show you everything Paso Robles has to offer — but that's
      also a very good reason to come back.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
